\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Rooted-tree Haar coordinates and quotient measure}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let a finite connected oriented graph have root #{o} and
spanning tree #{T}, and let #{G} be compact Hausdorff.
For each vertex #{v}, let #{t_v(U)} be the ordered tree-path
transport from #{o} to #{v}, with #{t_o=e}. For each
non-tree edge define
##{H_e=t_{t(e)}^{-1}U_et_{s(e)}.}
Then
##{\Phi_T:U\longmapsto
 ((t_v)_{v\ne o},(H_e)_{e\notin T})}
is a homeomorphism carrying product Haar to product Haar.
There are #{|V|-1} tree variables and
#{b_1=|E|-|V|+1} chord variables.}

\p{Under a vertex gauge transformation #{g},
##{t_v(g\cdot U)=g_vt_v(U)g_o^{-1},\qquad
 H_e(g\cdot U)=g_oH_e(U)g_o^{-1}.}
Consequently the orbit identification of \ref{fdip-006F}
carries the Haar quotient measure to
#{q_*\mu_G^{\otimes b_1}}, where
#{q:G^{b_1}\to G^{b_1}/G} is simultaneous conjugacy.
For a nonnegative integrable, pointwise gauge-invariant
Borel weight #{w}, put
#{\widetilde w(H)=w(\Phi_T^{-1}((e)_{v\ne o},H))}.
It is constant on simultaneous conjugacy classes, and the
weighted quotient measure is
##{q_*(\widetilde w\,\mu_G^{\otimes b_1}).}
Quotient measurable sets here are those with Borel preimage;
this includes the Borel sets of the topological quotient.}

\proof{
\p{Set #{H_e=e} on tree edges. The inverse map is
##{U_e=t_{t(e)}H_et_{s(e)}^{-1}.}
The unique rooted paths verify the inverse on the tree, and
the chord formula verifies it on the remaining edges.
Both directions are continuous. Order the non-root vertices
so that a parent precedes its children. If a tree edge has
parent #{p} and child #{v}, its path variable is
#{t_v=U_e^\epsilon t_p}, with the appropriate orientation
#{\epsilon}. Replacing that edge coordinate by #{t_v} is a
replacement of \ref{fdip-00MR}, using already computed parent
coordinates. After all tree replacements, change each chord
coordinate to #{t_{t(e)}^{-1}U_et_{s(e)}}. Each chord is an
independent pivot; all tree coordinates remain fixed.
Thus \ref{fdip-00MR} proves the product-Haar assertion.}

\p{Endpoint cancellation from \ref{fdip-00MP} gives the tree
transport rule. Substitution in the chord formula leaves
only #{g_o} and #{g_o^{-1}}. Gauging by #{g_v=t_v^{-1}}
sets all tree values to #{e}; a transformation between two
such representatives must be constant on the connected tree.
It therefore acts by simultaneous conjugation on the chords,
as already proved in \ref{fdip-006F}.}

\p{For the stated pointwise invariant weight,
#{w(U)=\widetilde w(H(U))}. The projection onto chord variables
has Haar pushforward, so integrating any nonnegative measurable
function of #{H} gives its product-Haar integral, without
an extra orbit-volume factor. Applying this to
#{\widetilde w\,1_{q^{-1}(A)}} proves the quotient formula.
All stabilizers are retained by this pushforward: the orbit
of a chord tuple has stabilizer equal to the common centralizer
of its entries. For #{b_1=0}, the chord space and quotient
are points.}

\p{For compact metrizable #{G}, a weight given only as an
invariant #{L^1} class can first be replaced by a pointwise
invariant Borel representative using the averaging argument
of \ref{fdip-00MQ}, applied to the identity map. The quotient
formula then applies to that representative.}
}

\p{Driver's tree integration theorem already treats arbitrary
finite oriented graphs, with a rooted forest, for connected
compact Lie groups
\citet{Theorem 5.3 and Corollary 5.4, pp. 590–591}{driver1989ym2}.
The explicit substitutions above establish the stated wider
compact-group applicability.}
